use std
into cfbmk.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace cfbmk). It is a small writer of Compound File Binary files, used only by the tests to build invented files in memory (the .msg tests and the CFB edge cases need files that no office program here can write). It lays streams out the way the format asks: streams under 4096 bytes in the mini stream, the others in regular sectors, a FAT, a mini FAT, a DIFAT for FAT sector numbers beyond the 109 the header holds, and a balanced sibling tree for every storage. Every module-level name starts with `cm_` or `CM_`.

`CM_NO` is 0xFFFFFFFF, `CM_END` is 0xFFFFFFFE, `CM_FAT` is 0xFFFFFFFD and `CM_DIF` is 0xFFFFFFFC.

`struct CmEnt` has `nat int`, `nlen int` (the entry's own name, ASCII, in `cm_pool`), `pat int`, `plen int` (its full path in `cm_pool`), `parent int` (the entry number of its storage), `kind int` (1 storage, 2 stream, 5 root), `dat int`, `dlen int` (a stream's bytes in `cm_pool`), `left int`, `right int`, `child int`, `start int`.

Module state: `var cm_pool byte[>..]` and `var cm_ents CmEnt[>..]`; `var cm_sib int[>..]` is a scratch list.

`cm_le(out byte[>..]&, v int, n int)` appends v to out as n little-endian bytes.

`cm_reset()` clears `cm_pool` and `cm_ents` and pushes the root entry: kind 5, name "Root Entry", empty path, parent -1, child `CM_NO`.

`cm_find(path const byte[:]) int` is the entry number of the entry whose path equals path byte for byte, or -1.

`cm_add(path const byte[:], data const byte[:])` adds a stream. The path has slashes between storage names, for example "Dir/Mid". For every proper prefix of the path (the text before each slash) that has no entry yet, push a storage entry (kind 1) whose parent is the entry of the prefix before it (the root for the first), then push the stream entry (kind 2) with the last name, the full path, its parent, and its bytes copied into `cm_pool`. Every entry gets left, right and child `CM_NO`, and start 0.

`cm_before(a int, b int) bool` is true when entry a sorts before entry b in a directory tree: the shorter name first, equal lengths by upper-cased bytes.

`cm_tree(lo int, hi int) int` builds a balanced binary tree from the sorted entry numbers `cm_sib[lo]` up to and including `cm_sib[hi]` and returns the entry number at its top: `CM_NO` when lo is above hi; otherwise the middle one (lo plus hi, divided by 2), with its left set to `cm_tree` of the lower half and its right to `cm_tree` of the upper half.

`cm_link()` gives every storage and the root a child tree: for each entry that is a storage or the root, collect into `cm_sib` the entries whose parent is it, sort them with an insertion sort by `cm_before`, and set its child to `cm_tree(0, count minus 1)` (`CM_NO` when there are none).

`cm_build(v4 bool, hdr int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and writes the whole file. v4 chooses 4096-byte sectors and major version 4, otherwise 512-byte sectors and version 3. hdr is how many FAT sector numbers go in the header (at most 109); any others go in DIFAT sectors. Let ssz be the sector size and epf be ssz divided by 4. Call `cm_link()`. Then lay out, giving each stream entry its start: a stream of 0 bytes starts at `CM_END`; a stream shorter than 4096 bytes lives in the mini stream, in 64-byte mini sectors numbered from 0 in entry order, each stream taking its length divided by 64 rounded up of them, and its start is its first mini sector; every other stream takes its length divided by ssz rounded up regular sectors and starts at its first one. Regular sector numbers run from 0 in this order: the sectors of the large streams in entry order; the mini stream (the mini sectors in order, padded with zeros to whole sectors; root start is its first sector, or `CM_END` when there is none); the mini FAT (the 4-byte chain entries of every mini sector, padded to whole sectors with `CM_NO`: each stream's mini sectors chain to the next and the last holds `CM_END`); the directory (128 bytes per entry, padded to whole sectors with unused entries: zero bytes except left, right and child `CM_NO`); the DIFAT sectors; the FAT sectors. The number of FAT sectors is the smallest n for which n times epf is at least the count of all the other sectors plus n; and when n is above hdr add the number of DIFAT sectors, which is (n minus hdr) divided by (epf minus 1) rounded up, to the count first (so find n by trying 1, 2, 3 and so on). The FAT has n times epf entries: chains of large streams (each sector holds the next, the last `CM_END`), the mini stream and the mini FAT and the directory likewise, every DIFAT sector holds `CM_DIF`, every FAT sector `CM_FAT`, all others `CM_NO`. The header is 512 bytes padded with zeros to ssz: the eight signature bytes D0 CF 11 E0 A1 B1 1A E1, 16 zero bytes, minor version 0x3E (2 bytes), major version 3 or 4 (2), byte order 0xFFFE (2), sector shift 9 or 12 (2), mini sector shift 6 (2), 6 zero bytes, the number of directory sectors (4; 0 for version 3), the FAT sector count (4), the first directory sector (4), 0 (4), the mini stream cutoff 4096 (4), the first mini FAT sector (4, `CM_END` when none), the mini FAT sector count (4), the first DIFAT sector (4, `CM_END` when none), the DIFAT sector count (4), and 109 DIFAT entries: the first min of hdr and n FAT sector numbers (the FAT sectors are the last n sectors) and `CM_NO` for the rest. A DIFAT sector holds epf minus 1 FAT sector numbers (those beyond the header's, `CM_NO` for unused) and then the number of the next DIFAT sector (`CM_END` for the last). A directory entry is: the name in UTF-16 little endian padded with zeros to 64 bytes, the name length in bytes including the two zero bytes (2), the type (1), the color byte 1, left (4), right (4), child (4), 16 zero bytes, 4 zero bytes, 16 zero bytes of times, the start sector (4) and the size (4, then 4 zero bytes); the root's size is the mini stream's length in bytes (the number of mini sectors times 64), storages have size 0 and start 0. Then write the sectors in number order after the header.
